<options source="ScorchDroid">
  <!-- M2 vertical slice: a fast local practice-vs-bots game. This is a
       ScorchDroid-owned config, not part of upstream's data/ - see the
       porting plan (AssetDataExtractor / initEngine). -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfMinPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M5 Phase 2: OptionsGame.cpp's own default NoMaxPlayers is 2, which
       addHumanTank() + the single starting bot already fill on their own -
       ServerConnectAuthHandler::processMessageInternal() rejects
       ("Server full") any real network client trying to join once
       getNoMaxPlayers() - getNoOfTanks() <= 0, found the hard way testing
       a real two-emulator join. Raised well past 2 so a host still has
       room for real LAN players, not just its own local bot-practice tank. -->
  <option>
    <name>NumberOfPlayers</name>
    <value>8</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>RemoveBotsAtPlayers</name>
    <value>2</value>
  </option>
  <!-- M4: engine_jni.cpp's addHumanTank() adds one real (destinationId != 0)
       "my tank" player itself, counted by
       ServerStateEnoughPlayers::countBots() exactly like a real network
       client would be - so only one more bot slot is needed to reach the
       2-player total above. PlayerType1 left as "Human" (the OptionsGame.cpp
       default) so ballanceBots() doesn't also try to add a bot into that
       slot. -->
  <option>
    <name>PlayerType2</name>
    <value>Moron</value>
  </option>
  <!-- TurnSimultaneous (0): every tank can move/fire within the same round
       window, rather than strict turn order (the default,
       TurnSequentialLoserFirst) - simplifies touch-fire, which just directly
       submits a move for whichever tank is tapped, with no turn-order
       bookkeeping of our own. -->
  <option>
    <name>TurnType</name>
    <value>0</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>ShotTime</name>
    <value>35</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>BuyingTime</name>
    <value>30</value>
  </option>
  <option>
    <name>StartTime</name>
    <value>5</value>
  </option>
</options>
